PART 1 - IDENTIFYING THE RACE CONDITION The bug you're encountering in the debounced auto-save flow of your JavaScript module can be traced back to a race condition. A race condition happens when two or more code paths compete for control, leading to inconsistent behavior if they interfere with each other's execution. In this case, it arises from the interleaving of tasks that rely on the same data source. To understand why this bug occurs under rapid edits: 1. Rapid edits by multiple users trigger simultaneous events on the client-side.
2. The debounced auto-save function is designed to periodically check if any changes have been made since the last save operation.
3. If these checks happen in quick succession, they might occur during different edit intervals and access inconsistent state. PART 2 - FIXING THE RACE CONDITION To correct this issue and ensure consistent behavior under concurrent edits: 1. Instead of debouncing the auto-save function, defer its execution to a separate process that runs after all client-side operations have completed. ```javascript
function queueAutoSave() { const promises = [ // Code to execute before saving ]; return Promise.all(promises).then(() => { // Now it's safe to call your existing debounced save function debounceSave(); });
}
``` 2. Make sure that the debounced save function only executes after all client-side operations are finished, not during them. ```javascript
function debounceSave() { return new Promise((resolve) => { const timeout = setTimeout(() => resolve(), DEBOUNCE_DELAY); // Clear timeout on cleanup window.addEventListener('beforeunload', () => clearTimeout(timeout)); });
}
``` 3. Alternatively, you can use a library like `redux-thunk` or `mobx-observed-effects` to ensure that the action of saving only happens after all side effects have been processed. 4. Finally, if your debounced save operation relies on the state being in a consistent read-write mode: ```javascript
